ID: Ch9-Intro
TEXT:

 

The leopard watches us from the crumbled archway of the ruins, tail twitching against the stone, before turning back into the shadows. We shoulder our packs and head out under the canopy. Flower takes the lead, but the spring in her step has vanished. Her shoulders stay tight, eyes darting from the dirt path to the thickets on either side.

"Right," she says, her voice lacking its usual bounce. "We follow this trail for a few hours until the crossroads. After that, the path runs out. You'll need to stay right behind me if you don't want to wander off into the brush. If anyone needs a breather or some food, speak up and we'll—"

"Flower," Kate cuts in, matching her stride. "Why did you agree to come with us to the ogre fort? Walking into monster territory without backup isn't exactly safe, even for you."

"Well... yes," Flower mumbles. "But if I didn't come, Eleya would have torn you all to pieces."

"And?" Kate's voice stays flat. "If the fox killed us, none of it would have been on your conscience. Why stick your neck out for strangers? Why not let her have us and walk away?"

Flower looks at the ground, kicking a pebble into the ferns. "I don't know. I just... I couldn't."

Kate studies the side of the girl's face for a long moment. The tension in her jaw eases, and the faintest ghost of a smile touches her mouth.

"I see," Kate says. "It seems we got off on the wrong foot this morning. Let's fix that."

She extends a hand toward Flower.

"I'm Kate. Good to meet you."

Flower blinks at the outstretched hand, caught off guard, before giving it an awkward, tentative shake.

"I'm Rose," Rose says, stepping up with a warm smile and offering her hand next. "We're glad to have you with us, Flower."

"Hadrik," the dwarf rumbles, giving a mock salute. "Or just 'dwarf.' Either works."

Flower stumbles back half a step, hands flying up in sudden distress.

"I'm sorry!" she squeaks. "No one's ever really... invited me into a group before. What am I supposed to say? What do people say?"

"Nothing," Kate tells her gently. "You don't have to say a thing."

Flower exhales, the panic dropping from her face. "Oh. Good. That's much easier."

choice("Introduce myself to Flower as well", Ch9-Introduce, v_current_scene = Ch9-Introduce, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)
choice("Keep my silence for now", Ch9-Silence, v_current_scene = Ch9-Silence, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: Ch9-Introduce
TEXT:

 "I'm Barry," I tell her. "Pleased to meet you as well!"

"Oh, thanks!" Flower says, perking up. "I caught yours back in the cell. Hard to forget that one."

"Understatement of the century," Hadrik snorts. "Throw a stone in the Western Continent and you'll brain three Barrys before it hits the dirt."

He bellows a laugh that sends a flock of thrushes scattering from the treetops.

"Is it really that common over there?" Flower asks me.

"I've met two dozen without trying," I say, "and I don't go out of my way to meet people."

"If we're keeping score," Hadrik says, "my clan named every third boy Hadrik on the Northern Continent. Standard giant stock."

Flower stops in her tracks, staring up at his beard. "You're a giant? Wow. You must've been the absolute runt of the litter."

Hadrik lets out another booming roar.

"Not quite, lass. The clan had plenty to say about me, but height wasn't the complaint. I'm wearing a dwarf's skin because of a shapeshifting trick. In my proper skin, I clear most roofs."

"I've been wondering," Rose says, tilting her head. "Why a dwarf? Wouldn't a human shape keep your proportions closer to what you're used to in a fight?"

"Ever hear what my mates call me?" Hadrik winks. "'The dwarf.' I've spent decades in Northern taprooms drinking dwarven ale. I talk like them, swear like them, and drink like them. Shifting into a dwarf raised fewer questions than pretending to be an oversized human. Besides, fighting with short limbs is a proper handicap. Keeps things interesting."

He chuckles, slapping his stout thigh.

Flower drifts toward Daren, who has been walking several paces ahead in stiff silence.

"I didn't get yours," she says softly.

"Daren," he replies, eyes fixed on the tree line. "Just Daren."

"You don't like me much, do you?"

"You're fine," Daren says evenly. "It's the creature sharing your skull I have a problem with."

Hadrik groans. "Lighten up, Daren! What's the worst she does? Shake hands with the ogres and lead an ambush?"

"Precisely," Daren says without looking back.

Flower's shoulders drop. "I see why Petal refuses to talk to you."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Awakening, v_current_scene = Ch9-Awakening)


ID: Ch9-Silence
TEXT:

 I keep my mouth shut and hang back. Daren is lingering near the rear as well, his plate armor clinking with every step. He drops a heavy gauntlet onto my shoulder and leans in.

"I don't trust that banshee, Barry," he murmurs. "The child seems harmless enough, but that doesn't mean the spirit coiled inside her shares the sentiment. Keep your eyes open."

Flower drops back toward us, glancing up at Daren's stern profile.

"I didn't get yours," she says softly.

"Daren," he replies, eyes fixed on the path ahead. "Just Daren."

"You don't like me much, do you?"

"You're fine," Daren says evenly. "It's the creature sharing your skull I have a problem with."

Hadrik groans from up ahead. "Lighten up, Daren! What's the worst she does? Shake hands with the ogres and lead an ambush?"

"Precisely," Daren says without looking back.

Flower sighs. "I see why Petal refuses to talk to you." She glances my way. "Aren't you going to tell me your name?"

"I figured you'd ask," I say.

"Oh, no, I already know yours, Barry! Hard to forget that one."

"Understatement of the century," Hadrik snorts, turning around. "Throw a stone in the Western Continent and you'll brain three Barrys before it hits the dirt."

He bellows a laugh that echoes through the trees.

"Is it really that common over there?" Flower asks me.

"I've met two dozen without trying," I say, "and I don't go out of my way to meet people."

"If we're keeping score," Hadrik says, "my clan named every third boy Hadrik on the Northern Continent. Standard giant stock."

Flower stops in her tracks, staring up at his beard. "You're a giant? Wow. You must've been the absolute runt of the litter."

Hadrik lets out another booming roar.

"Not quite, lass. The clan had plenty to say about me, but height wasn't the complaint. I'm wearing a dwarf's skin because of a shapeshifting trick. In my proper skin, I clear most roofs."

"I've been wondering," Rose says, tilting her head. "Why a dwarf? Wouldn't a human shape keep your proportions closer to what you're used to in a fight?"

"Ever hear what my mates call me?" Hadrik winks. "'The dwarf.' I've spent decades in Northern taprooms drinking dwarven ale. I talk like them, swear like them, and drink like them. Shifting into a dwarf raised fewer questions than pretending to be an oversized human. Besides, fighting with short limbs is a proper handicap. Keeps things interesting."

He chuckles, slapping his stout thigh.

choice("Continue", Ch9-Awakening, v_current_scene = Ch9-Awakening)


ID: Ch9-Awakening
TEXT:

#if(v_ch8_old == 0) {
 "If you don't mind me asking," Kate says, falling into step beside her, "how did you and Petal end up fused? I've never heard of two souls sharing one frame."

"Oh, Petal told me our souls tangled together during her awakening," Flower says cheerfully. "Twenty years ago, give or take. I don't know the technical details. You'd have to ask her."

Hadrik stops, scratching at his beard. "Hold on. Twenty years ago? How old are you, lass?"

"Turning thirty next month!" she says, beaming.

We all stare at the four-foot girl in acrobat's trousers.

"The body stops aging once a banshee settles in," she explains, unfazed by our silence. "I was a few weeks shy of ten when Petal woke up."

Rose presses a hand to her chest, her expression softening with pity. "I'm so sorry, Flower... That must have been a horrifying thing for a child to endure."

"What? No, you have it completely backward!" Flower waves her hands frantically. "The day Petal woke up was the best day of my entire life! For the first time ever, I had someone in there with me. You have no idea what it's like to spend years completely alone inside your own head."

}
