"What? No, you have it completely backward!" Flower waves her hands frantically. "The day Petal woke up was the best day of my entire life! For the first time ever, I had someone in there with me. You have no idea what it's like to spend years completely alone inside your own head."

}
choice(""If someone else is living in there, I need to know which of you is holding the amulet."", Ch9-Space-out, v_current_scene = Ch9-Space-out)
choice(""You just described a housemate who can execute you."", Ch9-Imagination, v_current_scene = Ch9-Imagination)
choice(""So when you argue with yourself, there's actually someone arguing back? That's terrifyingly efficient."", Ch9-Doctor, v_current_scene = Ch9-Doctor)


ID: Ch9-Space-out
TEXT:

 "If someone else is living in there, I need to know which of you is holding the amulet."

"I am!" Flower stomps a bare foot against the pine needles, kicking up dry loam. "I've held this body since the day I was born! Why does everyone assume I'm just sitting in the passenger seat?"

"I don't," Kate says quietly.

Flower slows her pace, blinking up at her. "You don't?"

"No. You aren't the first person I've encountered with that detachment." Kate keeps her stride long and even, her gaze scanning the birch trunks ahead. "A girl I grew up with—Diane—experienced the world the exact same way. Several of the children at the Beacon of Hope did. The lessathi overseers called them ethereals: mortals whose consciousness anchors primarily on the ethereal plane, experiencing existence from the soul outward rather than through nerve endings."

Rose knits her brow, stepping over an exposed pine root. "From the soul outward?"

"Most mortals interact with the world through physical sensation," Kate explains, gesturing with a gloved hand. "They rarely perceive the architecture of their own spirit. But the soul—the vessel of will, memory, and personal identity—inhabits an ethereal pocket we call the mind. The physical brain is merely an organic translator between spirit and muscle, not the source of thought. When flesh dies and decomposes in the earth, the soul eventually dissolves back into raw ethereal matter. But ethereals feel that separation while they breathe. To them, the physical frame feels like an apparatus—a massive wooden puppet where you have to consciously pull cables to swing an arm or frame a sentence."

"I pretend I have invisible cords tied to my fingers and toes!" Flower chimes in with a bright, sunny beam. "It makes walking across rough ground feel much less clumsy."

"Ordinary children notice that mechanical detachment instantly," Kate continues, her voice darkening slightly. "They ostracize ethereal children before they even understand why. That isolation forces ethereals inward. Some withdraw into complete silence. Others develop hyper-focused proficiencies to bridge the gap. Diane would sit for three straight days disassembling complex lessathi clockwork without looking up or eating. Flower, I take it your circus tumbling was yours?"

"Every single day!" Flower beams, bouncing lightly on her heels. "Balance, vaults, aerial flips—I practiced until my fingers didn't need cords to know where the air was!"

choice("Continue", Ch9-Beacon, v_current_scene = Ch9-Beacon)


ID: Ch9-Imagination
TEXT:

 "You just described a housemate who can execute you."

"Children come up with wild stories to keep themselves occupied," Hadrik chuckles, adjusting his axe strap. "Why, back in the clan, I knew a lad who swore a mountain troll lived behind his teeth and ate his breakfast."

"I'm not making it up!" Flower shouts, stomping her bare heel into the dirt. "I lived in that quiet for ten years before she arrived! Why does everyone treat it like a bedtime tale?"

"I don't," Kate says, her voice level.

Flower looks up, her eyes wide with surprise. "You believe me?"

"I do. I grew up with survivors who bore the same condition." Kate snaps a dry birch twig between her fingers, tossing the splintered bark into the ferns. "The lessathi called them ethereals. Most mortals live entirely within their skin, perceiving the world through touch and blood. But an ethereal lives from the soul. To an ethereal, the physical body is an awkward shell, and the internal mind is a vast, tangible space on the ethereal plane."

Rose tilts her head, listening intently as our boots crunch over the gravel. "A tangible space?"

"An inhabited one," Kate says. "When an ordinary mortal undergoes an awakening, the sudden arrival of a banshee triggers violent spiritual rejection because the host has no concept of their own spirit. But an ethereal already inhabits that internal fortress. When Petal awakened within Flower, she didn't find an empty vessel. She found another conscious soul already standing on the ramparts."

"That's why it wasn't terrifying!" Flower says, her small hands clasped tight. "I wasn't being invaded. After ten years of complete silence in the dark, somebody finally knocked on the door."

"That profound childhood isolation is why ethereals develop such obsessive disciplines," Kate continues. "They fixate on single proficiencies where soul and action align without social friction. Diane poured her life into clockwork and lessathi mechanisms. You poured yours into acrobatics and balance."

"Acrobatics and tumbling!" Flower beams, the gloom vanishing from her face. "The circus master said I learned to walk on high wires before I could run on flat grass!"

choice("Continue", Ch9-Beacon, v_current_scene = Ch9-Beacon)


ID: Ch9-Doctor
TEXT:

 "So when you argue with yourself, there's actually someone arguing back? That's terrifyingly efficient."

"That's a grim way to look at a child's head," Hadrik rumbles, shaking his head. "Spend three months snowbound in a Northern drift and you'll hold full debates with your boots. Doesn't mean the leather's talking back."

"It's not arguing!" Flower shouts, stomping a bare foot on a mossy stone. "Petal and I understand each other completely! Why does everyone make it sound like madness?"

"I don't," Kate says, pausing briefly near a granite outcrop to check our rear trail before stepping back into formation.

Flower blinks in astonishment. "You don't think I'm crazy?"

"No. I grew up with orphans who experienced the exact same cognitive reality." Kate keeps her eyes on the path ahead, her voice steady and measured. "The lessathi at the Beacon studied them extensively, naming them ethereals. For an ordinary person, the mind is simply the internal murmur of the physical brain. But the lessathi texts proved that the soul anchors on the ethereal plane, and the brain is merely a biological translator. For an ethereal, consciousness operates directly from the soul. When two complete spiritual entities fuse into a single frame, they share that conduit. An internal conversation is not an echo—it is two minds sharing an immediate, unfiltered channel."

"Exactly!" Flower nods eagerly. "We don't even need words half the time. It's like thoughts just pass between us like water!"

"Because ethereals operate from the soul, they process complex spatial and structural systems without the usual physical distraction," Kate continues. "It produces singular, intense proficiencies from an early age. Diane could decipher lessathi mechanical blueprints at eight years old. Flower, I imagine your acrobatics developed under that same singular focus?"

"I tumbled until sunset every day!" Flower smiles proudly. "High wires, trapeze swings, handstands on wagon wheels—I never felt clumsy when I was moving!"

choice("Continue", Ch9-Beacon, v_current_scene = Ch9-Beacon)


ID: Ch9-Beacon
TEXT:

 "Your acrobatics are notable," Kate says, "but they pale in comparison to your affinity for magic. Do you know why that is?"

"Petal says I pick up spells faster than any human has a right to," Flower says, tilting her head. "Is that because I'm an ethereal too?"

"Yes. The ethereal plane borders the magical plane far more closely than the material realm does. Living from the soul gives you an intuitive grasp of magical structures that the rest of us have to study years to comprehend. Being an ethereal doesn't grant you magic if you aren't born with the spark, but an ethereal born as a mage becomes a prodigy. That is why the lessathi at the Beacon collected ethereal orphans—to increase the survival rate of their experiments. Diane and her brother were both ethereals, and their progress eclipsed everyone else."

"That explains why Petal looked so stunned when she first broke into my mind," Flower murmurs.

"A banshee expecting a defenseless human soul walked into an occupied fortress," Kate says. "You had home-ground advantage and were already aware of your own spirit. She couldn't purge you, so she was forced to settle for a stalemate."

Flower stops dead in her tracks, her eyes widening.

"The Beacon... the experiments..." She looks at Kate with sudden clarity. "Are you an artificial mage?"

"I am," Kate answers flatly.

"Oh gods." Flower's face crumbles with sympathy. "You had it so much worse than I did. At least I had Petal's instincts when the hunters came for us. She taught me how to slip patrols and survive in the wilds. Would you like me to show you some of her tricks?"

Kate hesitates, then gives a stiff nod. "I suppose that wouldn't hurt."

"Great!" The bright grin returns to Flower's face. "I'll go over everything I remember on the march. Come on, we're losing daylight!"

From the golden amulet hanging against Flower's chest, a raspy, multi-toned whisper mutters under its breath:

"Ethereals, is it? Sounds delightfully pretentious. I think I'll adopt the title myself."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Crossroads, v_current_scene = Ch9-Crossroads)


ID: Ch9-Crossroads
TEXT:

 The miles slip behind us as the terrain roughens. Slate outcroppings pierce the loam, and the pines thin out into pale birch groves choked with strange, violet-veined ferns that curl away from our boots. Daren and Hadrik spend the morning trying to outdo each other with tavern boasts—Daren narrating noble standoffs against bandit hordes with practiced gravity, Hadrik interrupting with bellowing accounts of bar brawls that leveled two-story inns and ended in shared tankards. Ahead of us, Kate and Flower walk shoulder to shoulder, speaking in low, steady murmurs as they trade stories of living on the run.

By the time the sun climbs overhead, burning the mist off the valleys, the dirt path widens and splits into a rutted four-way crossroads. We halt in the clearing. Daren wipes sweat from his brow and turns to Rose, his face settling into grave lines.

"Rose, you should take the eastern fork and head for your village," he says. "Don't concern yourself with the collars. We'll handle the fort. The sick back home are waiting on those herbs."

"She can't," Kate says before Rose can answer. "Remember what the leopard told us? The lessathi built a tether into these collars. If any two drift more than three miles apart, the enchantments begin discharging electrical shocks through our flesh. Past four miles, the coils fry our hearts. Rose... I am sorry. If I had known this would happen, I never would have let you follow me yesterday. This is my doing."

"Kate, stop," Rose says gently, placing a hand on Kate's arm. "You had no way of anticipating any of this. And with the direct route we've been taking, I'll still reach home ahead of my original timeline. Don't carry guilt that isn't yours. We're friends."

Kate stares down at Rose's hand, swallowing hard before giving a tight nod. 

We drop our packs near a ring of mossy boulders to eat a quick midday ration. Flower, who skips her share of dried meat, wanders over to a pile of slate fragments by the wayside. With flickers of orange light from her fingertips, she begins blasting the rocks into smoking gravel, grinning each time the stone shatters.

"You really don't miss a chance to train, do you?" I ask, walking over.

"Training?" She glances back, eyes bright. "I'm not training. I just like blowing things up!"

"How old did you say you were again?"

"Turning thirty!"

choice(""You died at ten and kept the voice. I need to know whether the judgment died with it."", Ch9-Acting, v_current_scene = Ch9-Acting)
choice(""If the body stopped at ten, I can't take your calls like an adult's. Not in a fight."", Ch9-Developed, v_current_scene = Ch9-Developed)


ID: Ch9-Acting
TEXT:

 "You died at ten and kept the voice. I need to know whether the judgment died with it."

"Your face acts like a ten-year-old!" she shoots back, puffing her cheeks into a petulant scowl.

I decide against debating that particular masterpiece of rhetoric.

"Where's Arraka?" I ask, looking at her bare collarbones. "You took off the amulet."

"She wanted to socialize," Flower says, pointing across the road. "I lent her to Hadrik. He's the only one who doesn't recoil when she opens her mouth."

Across the clearing, Hadrik sits on an overturned log with the golden locket resting casually in his palm.

"Shame I can't take my proper size at the stronghold," the dwarf is saying, stroking his beard. "Nothing quite like the crunch of an ogre under a fifty-foot heel."

"At least you'll get to watch their vacant faces when you bat their fireballs back down their throats," Arraka's layered voice rasps from the metal.

"True enough," Hadrik chuckles. "Though an ogre's so thick between the ears, he'd be charcoal before he realized the flame was his."

Both of them burst into laughter at the exact same second—a booming giant's roar and a shrieking banshee cackle.

"You know," Arraka purrs, "teleportation is my specialty. Crack this clasp and I'll pluck a few ogres straight from their beds. We can have a lovely little warm-up right here. What do you say?"

Hadrik grins, tapping the gold casing. "Not today, old woman."

"Pity. Would've been entertaining."

Flower watches them with an earnest, sunny expression. "I'm glad she found someone to talk to. We don't have much in common, and Petal despises her."

"Which brings up an obvious question," I say. "Why drag her around at all?"

Kate steps up beside us, arms crossed tightly over her chest. "I want to know the same thing. Why not drop the locket to the bottom of a river and walk away? Her aura makes the hair on my neck stand up."

Flower's playful demeanor vanishes in an instant. Her jaw tightens.

"I can't leave her," she says, her voice dropping.

"Why not?" Kate demands.

"Because she'll break out. The amulet suppresses almost all of her power, but if she's left alone for a few hours, she can channel just enough energy to trigger a small blast and shatter the casing from within."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Leader, v_current_scene = Ch9-Leader)


ID: Ch9-Developed
TEXT:

 "If the body stopped at ten, I can't take your calls like an adult's. Not in a fight."

"La la la la la! I can't hear you!" Flower slaps her hands over her ears, stomping in a little circle.

"Point taken," I say, throwing my hands up. "Where's Arraka, anyway? You aren't wearing the amulet."

"She wanted to socialize," Flower says, dropping her hands and pointing across the road. "I lent her to Hadrik. He's the only one who doesn't recoil when she opens her mouth."

Across the clearing, Hadrik sits on an overturned log with the golden locket resting casually in his palm.

"Shame I can't take my proper size at the stronghold," the dwarf is saying, stroking his beard. "Nothing quite like the crunch of an ogre under a fifty-foot heel."

