choice("Continue", Ch9-Leader, v_current_scene = Ch9-Leader)


ID: Ch9-Leader
TEXT:

 "So what?" Kate says. "If she escapes in the middle of nowhere, she ceases to be your problem."

"No," Flower says grimly. "If she gets out, she becomes everyone's problem."

"She's that dangerous?" I ask.

"Have either of you heard of the Still Winter?"

"A few historical accounts," I tell her. "A brutal conflict between the lessathi and the stillwaters on this continent, roughly six centuries ago."

"That's the summary," Flower says. "What the histories leave out is that Arraka commanded the stillwater army."

Kate's eyes narrow. "A banshee leading stillwaters?"

"She was fused to a stillwater man," Flower explains, "the same way Petal is fused to me. As far as anyone knows, those are the only two instances of soul fusion in history. Based on what you said earlier, Kate, I'd wager that stillwater was an ethereal too."

"Wait," I interrupt. "Banshees can possess men? I thought they only targeted women."

"Banshees are female spirits," Kate explains, turning to me. "To minimize the violent rejection during an awakening, they seek out female hosts so the spiritual resonance aligns. But a spirit of Arraka's caliber wouldn't care about precautions."

"Exactly," Flower nods. "She tore across the land until it took the combined might of Eleya and the Creator to finally bring her down."

"The Creator?" I ask. "The founder of Varathia's civilization?"

"The same," Flower says. "Even together, they couldn't destroy her outright. Instead, they placed an ancient binding on her essence, forbidding her from possessing any humanoid vessel. For six hundred years, she was forced into wild animals—bodies far too fragile to channel her real power."

"Then why is she a threat to you?" I ask. "If the binding holds—"

"Because of my aura," Flower says quietly. "Fusing with Petal twisted my spiritual signature so heavily that the binding no longer registers me as human. To the spell, I'm an open door. If I threw the amulet into a swamp, Arraka would blast it open, hunt me down, and take this body. And if she gets her hands on our magic... it won't just be the end of me and Petal. It will be the end of Varathia."

A chill settles over the clearing.

"So you have to keep her in arm's reach," I say slowly. "Snapping her channels every few hours to keep the blast from forming. What happens when you sleep?"

choice("Continue", Ch9-Dispel, v_current_scene = Ch9-Dispel)


ID: Ch9-Dispel
TEXT:

 "Petal and I sleep in shifts," Flower says. "Our body technically never rests. Petal's been asleep in the back of my mind ever since we cleared the ruins."

"And if you're both knocked cold?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing. "In battle? A stray blow, a sleep curse?"

"We looked for wards that could automatically disrupt foreign casting every few hours," Flower says, "but dispelling requires conscious focus on the specific weave."

Without breaking stride in her explanation, Flower turns toward Hadrik, flicks two fingers at the amulet in his palm, and a faint blue spark snaps against the gold.

"Curse you, brat!" Arraka's voice shrills from the locket. "Do you have eyes in the back of your skull?!"

"See?" Flower turns back to us with a small shrug. "You have to actively aim at what she's building. Since automatic dispelling wouldn't work, Petal wove a failsafe into our lifeforce instead. If we're both unconscious, a pulse tries to shock us awake. If we stay under for more than two hours, the spell snaps our heartstrings."

I stare at her. "It kills you?"

"Instantly," Flower says with a calm smile. "Petal's design. It ensures we die cleanly rather than letting Arraka devour our souls and slaughter the continent."

Kate stares at her for a long second. "That is... remarkably cold."

"Petal is very pragmatic!" Flower says proudly.

"Clearly," Kate murmurs.

choice(""Speaking of Petal, how have you two been getting along these past twenty years?"", Ch9-Getting-along, v_current_scene = Ch9-Getting-along)
choice(""So, what's life with Arraka like?"", Ch9-Life-with-Arraka, v_current_scene = Ch9-Life-with-Arraka)
choice(""Would you say that Petal is the smartest of your group?"", Ch9-Smartest, v_current_scene = Ch9-Smartest)


ID: Ch9-Getting-along
TEXT:

 "Speaking of Petal, how have you two been getting along these past twenty years?"

"Oh, it's a blast!" Flower says, hopping over a fallen birch. "We have so much fun. Petal enjoys it too—well, mostly. It's hard to tell sometimes because she's perpetually grumpy and hates talking to people."

"I know the type," I say, glancing sideways at Kate.

Kate shoots me a frosty glare and says nothing.

"Flower!" Daren's voice booms from thirty yards ahead. "Is this the path?"

"No!" Flower yells, breaking into a sprint toward him. "If you keep going straight, you'll sink into a peat bog! We take the left ridge until the trees thin out!"

We adjust course, falling in behind her. Once clear of the ruins, Flower moves with total certainty, checking notched trunks and mossy cairns she must have laid years ago.

By dusk, the woods give way to a broad grassy valley carved by a shallow river. The open banks leave no cover for ambushes. Kate and Flower fan out to sweep the perimeter, while Daren and Hadrik haul canvas from their enchanted packs to raise camp. With the heavy lifting handled, Rose and I take a seat on a dry log near the riverbank.

"How are you holding up?" I ask quietly. "You looked shaken after the hydra. And the dragon yesterday didn't seem much easier on your nerves."

A flush rises in Rose's cheeks.

"You noticed..." she murmurs, ducking her head.

choice("Continue", Ch9-Whimpers, v_current_scene = Ch9-Whimpers)


ID: Ch9-Life-with-Arraka
TEXT:

 "So, what's life with Arraka like?"

"Not as terrible as you'd think," Flower says, hopping over a fallen birch. "She tries to cause trouble whenever she gets a window, but when things turn lethal, she actually helps. If I die, she loses her only chance at a humanoid host once she breaks the amulet. Keeping us breathing is in her best interest."

"Flower!" Daren's voice booms from thirty yards ahead. "Is this the path?"

"No!" Flower yells, breaking into a sprint toward him. "If you keep going straight, you'll sink into a peat bog! We take the left ridge until the trees thin out!"

We adjust course, falling in behind her. Once clear of the ruins, Flower moves with total certainty, checking notched trunks and mossy cairns she must have laid years ago.

By dusk, the woods give way to a broad grassy valley carved by a shallow river. The open banks leave no cover for ambushes. Kate and Flower fan out to sweep the perimeter, while Daren and Hadrik haul canvas from their enchanted packs to raise camp. With the heavy lifting handled, Rose and I take a seat on a dry log near the riverbank.

"How are you holding up?" I ask quietly. "You looked shaken after the hydra. And the dragon yesterday didn't seem much easier on your nerves."

A flush rises in Rose's cheeks.

"You noticed..." she murmurs, ducking her head.

choice("Continue", Ch9-Whimpers, v_current_scene = Ch9-Whimpers)


ID: Ch9-Smartest
TEXT:

 "Would you say that Petal is the smartest of your group?"

"Smarter than me, definitely," Flower says, hopping over a fallen birch. "Arraka plays the clown, but she's cunning when she cares to be. When Petal and Arraka put their heads together during a crisis, the schemes they hatch are downright terrifying."

"Flower!" Daren's voice booms from thirty yards ahead. "Is this the path?"

"No!" Flower yells, breaking into a sprint toward him. "If you keep going straight, you'll sink into a peat bog! We take the left ridge until the trees thin out!"

We adjust course, falling in behind her. Once clear of the ruins, Flower moves with total certainty, checking notched trunks and mossy cairns she must have laid years ago.

By dusk, the woods give way to a broad grassy valley carved by a shallow river. The open banks leave no cover for ambushes. Kate and Flower fan out to sweep the perimeter, while Daren and Hadrik haul canvas from their enchanted packs to raise camp. With the heavy lifting handled, Rose and I take a seat on a dry log near the riverbank.

"How are you holding up?" I ask quietly. "You looked shaken after the hydra. And the dragon yesterday didn't seem much easier on your nerves."

A flush rises in Rose's cheeks.

"You noticed..." she murmurs, ducking her head.

choice("Continue", Ch9-Whimpers, v_current_scene = Ch9-Whimpers)


ID: Ch9-Whimpers
TEXT:

 "Well, it would have been a bit difficult to ignore you, what with the whimpers and trembling and everything."

Rose gives my arm a light, playful tap with her knuckles. She pulls a small clay jar of herbal salve from her satchel, untying the stopper. The smell of crushed wintergreen and damp mint rises between us.

"Give me your hand," she murmurs.

Before I can answer, she takes my wrist. Her touch is careful, steady, her thumb smoothing the cool, fragrant balm over the raw scrapes on my knuckles from the ruins. Her fingers linger along the back of my hand, pressing gently into the tendon.

"I see why Kate punches you," she says with a small smile, keeping her eyes on my hand. "Thank you for asking, though. Giant beasts have always terrified me. Goblins and ogres I can handle—I've run into enough while gathering herbs on the outer roads. But dragons and hydras... I knew they roamed the wilds, but facing both in twenty-four hours was more than I ever expected. I don't know how Daren and Hadrik treat them like everyday chores."

She looks up, her face inches away, warm in the river breeze—then seems to realize how long she has been holding my hand, her thumb pausing against my skin. A faint flush rises along her collarbone as she gently lets go and caps the jar, tucking a stray lock of auburn hair behind her ear.

"This must be just as foreign to you, though. You told me you hadn't fought a goblin before entering Varathia. How do you stay so level-headed when the ground is coming apart under your boots?"

choice(""I'm not calm, it's just that I'm very good at hiding fear."", Ch9-Hiding-fear, v_current_scene = Ch9-Hiding-fear)
choice(""It's because I'm carrying this stat device with me."", Ch9-Stat-device, v_current_scene = Ch9-Stat-device)
choice(""I spent all my fear yesterday. There's none left over for today."", Ch9-Crazy, v_current_scene = Ch9-Crazy)


ID: Ch9-Hiding-fear
TEXT:

 "I'm not calm, it's just that I'm very good at hiding fear."

"Modesty doesn't suit you, Barry," Rose says, watching the river. "If you were merely hiding panic, you wouldn't have stood your ground against Tyrath to save all of us. You were gallant up there. The focus in your eyes before you faced the dragon made me feel safe. It drove the dread right out of my chest."

She catches herself, her cheeks turning a deeper shade of rose, and hastily turns her face toward the water.

choice("Continue", Ch9-Blushing, v_current_scene = Ch9-Blushing)


ID: Ch9-Stat-device
TEXT:

 "It's because I'm carrying this stat device with me."

Rose blinks in surprise. "There is a setting for courage? I'd pay handsomely for one of those."

"No, that's not what I mean. Just knowing I have tools to push past my normal physical limits gives me steady footing. It makes me feel capable of matching real mages, especially when I can draw on actual magic like I did against Tyrath."

Rose smiles softly. "Whatever the source, you were gallant up there. The focus in your eyes before you faced the dragon made me feel safe. It drove the dread right out of my chest."

She catches herself, her cheeks turning a deeper shade of rose, and hastily turns her face toward the water.

choice("Continue", Ch9-Blushing, v_current_scene = Ch9-Blushing)


ID: Ch9-Crazy
TEXT:

 "I spent all my fear yesterday. There's none left over for today."

Rose lets out a quiet laugh. "I suppose terror takes a back seat when you're reckless enough to enter a mage tournament without a single spell."

"Hey, the stat booster is genuine enchantment. Especially when it lets me channel real magic like I did against Tyrath."

Rose smiles softly. "Whatever the source, you were gallant up there. The focus in your eyes before you faced the dragon made me feel safe. It drove the dread right out of my chest."

She catches herself, her cheeks turning a deeper shade of rose, and hastily turns her face toward the water.

choice("Continue", Ch9-Blushing, v_current_scene = Ch9-Blushing)


ID: Ch9-Blushing
TEXT:

#if(v_ch6_flirt != 2 && v_ch6_flirt != 3 && v_ch6_flirt != 4) {
 I let the blush pass without drawing attention to it.

"There's something I wanted to ask in private," I say. "What do you make of Flower?"

Rose turns back, the color in her face returning to normal.

"In what sense?" she asks. "She seems cheerful, warm, and wonderfully straightforward. A delight to have around, really. Why do you ask? What do you think of her?"

}
#if(v_ch6_flirt == 3 || v_ch6_flirt == 4) {
 "You were blushing just now," I say. "I saw it."

"I was not!" Rose insists, turning back to show her cooling cheeks. "Your imagination is running wild. Can we change the subject?"

She meets my gaze, her brown eyes softening. "Please?"

I let the teasing drop.

"Fair enough. There's something I wanted to ask in private. What do you make of Flower?"

Rose looks slightly surprised by the pivot.

"In what sense?" she asks. "She seems cheerful, warm, and wonderfully straightforward. A delight to have around, really. Why do you ask? What do you think of her?"

}
#if(v_ch6_flirt == 2) {
 "You were blushing just now," I say. "Have you reconsidered your stance on me since our last talk?"

"Only regarding your combat prowess," Rose says, turning back with an amused arch of her brow. "Had your very first remark to me not been about watching me instead of the tree line, matters might have unfolded differently. As it stands, my initial assessment remains firm."

"Ouch," I mutter.

Rose chuckles softly. "Don't look so crestfallen, Barry. We come from different worlds. When this tournament concludes, you'll cross back to the Western Continent and I will remain in Varathia. Even if something blossomed, it could never last."

"True enough," I concede. "So... what do you make of Flower?"

Rose looks slightly surprised by the pivot.

"In what sense?" she asks. "She seems cheerful, warm, and wonderfully straightforward. A delight to have around, really. Why do you ask? What do you think of her?"

}
choice(""I find her a bit annoying, to be frank."", Ch9-Annoying, v_current_scene = Ch9-Annoying)
choice(""I just can't bring myself to trust her."", Ch9-Trust, v_current_scene = Ch9-Trust)
choice(""I was just thinking about what Daren said this morning."", Ch9-Morning, v_current_scene = Ch9-Morning)
choice(""I think she's pretty fun too. I was just curious to see if you thought the same."", Ch9-Curious, v_current_scene = Ch9-Curious)


ID: Ch9-Annoying
TEXT:

 "I find her a bit annoying, to be frank."

"How so?"

"She's turning thirty, but acts like a ten-year-old child bounding over logs," I say. "It's difficult to take her tactical judgment seriously when we're marching into an ogre garrison."

"Remember, Barry, she never had the chance to grow up inside a normal world," Rose says gently, resting the clay jar in her lap. "When a child's body freezes and she lives two decades on the run with an entity in her skull, playful energy is how she keeps from breaking under the weight. Underneath the silliness, she took this march to save our lives. Give her some grace."

"I can manage that," I admit.

"Good," Rose smiles. "And don't worry, your secret is safe with me. I won't tell her you grumbled."

"Flower isn't the primary concern anyway," I say. "It's the spirit sharing her skin. What Daren said this morning keeps ringing in my ears."

"I trust Petal," Rose says without hesitation. "Twenty years of sharing a heartbeat without betrayal is proof enough for me. No one maintains a mask that long without slipping."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Sleep, v_current_scene = Ch9-Sleep)


ID: Ch9-Trust
TEXT:

 "I just can't bring myself to trust her."

"Why?"

"Banshees don't exactly have a sterling reputation for integrity or keeping faith with mortals."

Rose gasps softly, nudging my shoulder with her elbow. "Shame on you, Barry! I thought you were more perceptive than that. Did you only extend your trust to Kate because she looked human?"

"Kate was different! I knew from the start she was an artificial mage, not an outworldly entity."

"And Petal has protected Flower through two decades of exile," Rose says firmly. "If Flower trusts her with her own flesh and soul, I trust her with our backs. Please, Barry. Give her a chance to prove herself before you judge her by her species."

I let out a breath. "Fine. Benefit of the doubt, for now. But I'll be watching."

"Thank you," Rose beams, her eyes warm. "You won't regret it."

"Let's hope not."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Sleep, v_current_scene = Ch9-Sleep)


ID: Ch9-Morning
TEXT:

 "I was just thinking about what Daren said this morning."

"About Petal turning on us at the stronghold?"

"About her selling us out or vanishing the second the fighting starts."

Rose watches the river water lap against the bank pebbles, the evening light catching the auburn in her hair. "Daren carries deep scars from his knightly vows on the Eastern continent. To him, every banshee is a monster from the scriptures. But Flower isn't a victim chained to a corpse. When she talks about Petal, her face lights up. They survived twenty years together. You don't spend two decades keeping someone alive just to betray them for an ogre's favor."

"A fair assessment," I admit. "Loyalty that survives twenty winters isn't something you discard on an impulse."

"Exactly," Rose smiles, tucking a stray lock behind her ear. "Now try to look less grim before Kate catches us brooding."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Sleep, v_current_scene = Ch9-Sleep)


ID: Ch9-Curious
TEXT:

 "I think she's pretty fun too. I was just curious to see if you thought the same."

"It's a pity Daren refuses to see it," Rose says.

"Can you blame him? Banshees aren't known for their high moral standards, and the girl shares her skin with one."

"She brings warmth into a party that was starting to feel like a war council," Rose says, leaning back against the weathered log with a soft chuckle. "Daren will come around once he sees her in a fight. It's hard to stay stone-faced around someone who skips while carrying an ancient banshee."

"Let's hope Daren's broadsword agrees before we reach the fort."

"It will," Rose says gently. "We're going to get through this, Barry. All of us."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Sleep, v_current_scene = Ch9-Sleep)


ID: Ch9-Sleep
TEXT:

 While Rose and I finish talking, Daren and Hadrik finish staking the last canvas tent. Kate and Flower return from the tree line, reporting clear perimeter ground, and join us near the embers.

With dawn promising a grueling trek, we turn in early. Flower volunteers for the opening watch and climbs the grassy knoll overlooking the bend of the river. As she brushes past me, I feel the shift—her warm, crackling presence recedes into silence, replaced by the heavy, chilled weight of an otherworldly aura.

The girl has gone under, leaving the banshee at the wheel.

If I want to take the measure of our second guide before we reach the ogre fort, this is the window.

"Aren't you turning in, Barry?" Rose asks, pausing by the flap of her tent.

choice("Tell her what I just noticed about Flower and Petal", Ch9-Inventory, v_current_scene = Ch9-Inventory, v_ch9_inventory = 0)
choice("Tell her to go on ahead because I need to do an inventory of my backpack's contents", Ch9-Inventory, v_current_scene = Ch9-Inventory, v_ch9_inventory = 1)


ID: Ch9-Inventory
TEXT:

#if(v_ch9_inventory == 0) {
 "I'm staying up for a bit," I say.

I step closer and whisper what I felt from Flower's aura, and that I intend to have a word with the banshee.

Rose's eyes light up. "That's a wonderful idea! Let me drop my pack in the tent and I'll come with you."

We make our way up the rise. The grass is damp with evening dew. Near the crest, Flower's body sits cross-legged, staring out over the dark valley. When she turns her head, the eyes burning back at us are not green, but a piercing, glacial blue.

"The girl is asleep," she says, her voice stripped of all warmth. "I can wake her if you brought vital news."

She holds a thick sandwich in both hands, interrupted mid-bite.

"We came to speak with you," I say.

Her gaze narrows. "Did you? Take a seat, then."

She takes a deliberate bite as we settle onto the turf.

"Am I right in assuming you're Petal?" Rose asks warmly, extending a hand. "I'm Rose. It's a pleasure."

"My name is Illuna," the banshee says, keeping both hands firmly on her bread. "Petal is an infantile label the child pinned on me when I refused to surrender my true name. She has refused to drop it for two decades."

Seeing the hand ignored, Rose lowers it without fuss and folds her hands in her lap.

"Lovely evening for a watch," I offer.

Illuna turns her cold stare onto me.

"Spare me the human pleasantries. I have never had patience for roundabout banter. You climbed this hill because you don't trust me alone above your camp. Speak plainly."

}
#if(v_ch9_inventory == 1) {
 "I need to double-check my rations and gear before I sleep," I tell Rose. "Daren's spatial enchantment makes it hard to feel what's at the bottom of the canvas, and I want to be certain nothing was left behind."

Rose smiles. "Of course. Don't stay up too late, Barry."

"Sleep well," I say as she ducks inside.

I wait until the lanterns in the tents dim, then start up the knoll. The grass is damp with evening dew. Near the crest, Flower's body sits cross-legged, staring out over the dark valley. When she turns her head, the eyes burning back at me are not green, but a piercing, glacial blue.

"The girl is asleep," she says, her voice stripped of all warmth. "I can wake her if you brought vital news."

She holds a thick sandwich in both hands, interrupted mid-bite.

"I came to speak with you," I say.

Her gaze narrows. "Did you? Take a seat, then."

She takes a deliberate bite as I settle onto the turf.

"Lovely evening for a watch," I offer.

Illuna turns her cold stare onto me.

"Spare me the human pleasantries. I have never had patience for roundabout banter. You climbed this hill because you don't trust me alone above your camp. Speak plainly."

}
choice(""Yeah, that's about right."", Ch9-Right, v_current_scene = Ch9-Right)
choice(""I wanted you away from Flower before I said this."", Ch9-Know, v_current_scene = Ch9-Know)
choice(""I pulled you off the trail because I don't trust what Flower isn't saying."", Ch9-Weather, v_current_scene = Ch9-Weather)


ID: Ch9-Right
TEXT:

 "Yeah, that's about right."

"Prudent," Illuna says, chewing slowly. "I wouldn't trust a banshee without ironclad proof either. I've had Arraka rattling around my neck for twenty years."

A muffled squeal of protest vibrates from the gold amulet.

"Silence, old hag," Illuna snaps. "No one solicited your commentary."

"You aren't even going to try, are you?" I ask.

"Try what?"

"To convince us we can rely on you."

She lets out a dry breath. "Why would I bother? We travel together on the child's whim. Once those collars are off, we part ways and never lay eyes on each other again. Believe whatever keeps your heart rate down. I will do the same."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Sandwich, v_current_scene = Ch9-Sandwich)


ID: Ch9-Know
TEXT:

 "I wanted you away from Flower before I said this."

"Then you are either cautious or calculating," Illuna says flatly. "No sensible mortal trusts a banshee on faith, and Flower's childish enthusiasm is hardly reliable testimony."

"You aren't even going to try, are you?" I ask.

"Try what?"

"To convince us we can rely on you."

She lets out a dry breath. "Why would I bother? We travel together on the child's whim. Once those collars are off, we part ways and never lay eyes on each other again. Believe whatever keeps your heart rate down. I will do the same."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Sandwich, v_current_scene = Ch9-Sandwich)


ID: Ch9-Weather
TEXT:

 "I pulled you off the trail because I don't trust what Flower isn't saying."

Illuna stares at me, her blue eyes devoid of amusement.

"Flower says everything that enters her head," she says. "It's the spirit behind her that warrants your suspicion. If that concludes your interrogation, leave me to finish my meal."

"You aren't even going to try, are you?" I ask.

"Try what?"

"To convince us we can rely on you."

She lets out a dry breath. "Why would I bother? We travel together on the child's whim. Once those collars are off, we part ways and never lay eyes on each other again. Believe whatever keeps your heart rate down. I will do the same."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Sandwich, v_current_scene = Ch9-Sandwich)


ID: Ch9-Sandwich
TEXT:

#if(v_ch9_inventory == 0) {
 "Where did you get that sandwich?" Rose asks, glancing at the girl's empty belt. "You didn't bring a pack up here."

"I shaped it from raw mana," Illuna says. She flicks her wrist, and two wrapped sandwiches materialize out of empty air, dropping neatly onto our laps. "Eat if you're hungry."

I look at the bread in my hands, hesitating.

"For heaven's sake," Illuna sighs, catching my look. "You suspect poison? Who wastes time brewing toxins in this day and age? Basic neutralizing draughts sell for ten copper in any border market, and passive wards are child's play. Surely you drank at least one broad-spectrum antitoxin before entering a lethal tournament? Eat the food or drop it in the dirt."

Rose lets out a soft chuckle. "Flower warned me you had a sharp tongue."

"Splendid," Illuna mutters. "What other intimate details did the child share while I rested?"

"She certainly didn't mention you could conjure meals from nothing," Rose says, impressed. "That kind of materialization is usually limited to high priests in the capital."

"I am thoroughly ashamed to admit that Flower taught me the weave."

Rose blinks. "Flower did?"

"The girl has an infuriating talent for grasping spectacle magic on sight," Illuna says with a grimace. "She watched Eleya weave a feast once, twenty years ago, and mimicked the formula the next second. Yet when I attempt to teach her foundational healing theory, it is like explaining lessathi syntax to a pack mule."

}
#if(v_ch9_inventory == 1) {
 A rustle in the grass heralds Rose's arrival as she crests the knoll.

"Marvellous," Illuna deadpans. "Another guest eager to discuss the clouds. I will never finish this crust."

Rose reaches us, breathing hard, and plants a firm punch into my shoulder.

"You're a terrible liar, Barry," she huffs. "Sorting your pack? Did you forget Daren enchanted my canvas too? If you tap the inside flap, the enchantment displays a glowing visual manifest of every item inside! You don't need to dig through anything."

I grimace inwardly.

"You could have told me," Rose adds softly. "Did you think I wouldn't want to meet her?"

"My mistake," I say.

Rose sighs, turning to the banshee. "Am I right in assuming you're Petal? I'm Rose. It's a pleasure."

"My name is Illuna," the banshee says, hands firmly on her food. "Petal is an infantile label the child pinned on me when I refused to surrender my true name. She has refused to drop it for two decades."

Rose smiles and sits on the grass beside us.

"Where did you get that sandwich anyway?" I ask. "You didn't bring a pack."

}
choice("Continue", Ch9-Multiplied, v_current_scene = Ch9-Multiplied) if (v_ch9_inventory == 0)
choice("Continue", Ch9-Magic, v_current_scene = Ch9-Magic) if (v_ch9_inventory == 1)


ID: Ch9-Magic
TEXT:

 "I shaped it from raw mana," Illuna says. She flicks her wrist, and two wrapped sandwiches materialize out of empty air, dropping neatly onto our laps. "Eat if you're hungry."

I look at the bread in my hands, hesitating.

"For heaven's sake," Illuna sighs, catching my look. "You suspect poison? Who wastes time brewing toxins in this day and age? Basic neutralizing draughts sell for ten copper in any border market, and passive wards are child's play. Surely you drank at least one broad-spectrum antitoxin before entering a lethal tournament? Eat the food or drop it in the dirt."

Rose lets out a soft chuckle. "Flower warned me you had a sharp tongue."

"Splendid," Illuna mutters. "What other intimate details did the child share while I rested?"

"She certainly didn't mention you could conjure meals from nothing," Rose says, impressed. "That kind of materialization is usually limited to high priests in the capital."

"I am thoroughly ashamed to admit that Flower taught me the weave."

Rose blinks. "Flower did?"

"The girl has an infuriating talent for grasping spectacle magic on sight," Illuna says with a grimace. "She watched Eleya weave a feast once, twenty years ago, and mimicked the formula the next second. Yet when I attempt to teach her foundational healing theory, it is like explaining lessathi syntax to a pack mule."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Multiplied, v_current_scene = Ch9-Multiplied)


ID: Ch9-Multiplied
TEXT:

 Rose smiles warmly.

"See?" she tells Illuna. "You can converse like a civilized person when you try."

Illuna scowls. "Triumph achieved. Now depart and leave me in peace."

"You have a very endearing scowl," Rose teases.

"Merciful gods," Illuna mutters, pressing a palm to her forehead. "Isn't dealing with Flower during daylight enough? Must I suffer her echoes at night?"

"Of course," Rose says, leaning forward. "Who else would keep you company?"

"The Flowers have multiplied..."

The hours drift past under the starlight. For all her abrasive retorts, Illuna doesn't leave, and beneath the barbed exterior, she slowly yields, offering dry, unvarnished recollections of their wanderings over the years.

Heavy footsteps crunch on the slope behind us. Daren and Hadrik crest the rise to relieve the watch.

Hadrik rubs his eyes. "Thought Flower was holding the hill alone."

"It appears Flower misled us," Daren says, his voice low and hard as iron. "She never intended to stand watch."

Illuna rises smoothly to her feet. The two of them lock eyes, the air between them turning brittle enough to snap.

choice(""We're all bound to the same errand here, right?"", Ch9-Calm, v_current_scene = Ch9-Calm)
choice(""Actually, their souls are fused, so Flower didn't technically lie yesterday."", Ch9-Fused, v_current_scene = Ch9-Fused)
choice(""You're not seriously going to start a fight here, are you?"", Ch9-Fight, v_current_scene = Ch9-Fight)


ID: Ch9-Calm
TEXT:

 "We're all bound to the same errand here, right?"

"Are we?" Daren's voice is low, his hand resting on the pommel of his broadsword. "You and Rose had the good sense to mount a watch, but left to herself, what stopped her from drawing a blade across our throats while we slept?"

"I have no need for petty assassination against disarmed mortals," Illuna says coldly. "You and your companions pose zero threat to me in your current state, healer. If you wish to test that assessment, take another step."

"I accept the invitation," Daren says, drawing his sword with a clean rasp of steel. "Let's see if your magic matches your arrogance."

"Daren, stop!" I bark, stepping squarely between them. "Are you planning to execute the girl just to get at the banshee?"

"The sword's purification enchantment can be focused manually," Daren insists, blade angled low. "If I concentrate, I can sever the spirit without harming the vessel."

"Have you lost your mind?" I shout. "If the weapon can't lock onto her aura on its own, you're guessing in the dark! You'll butcher them both!"

"Even if I fail, a clean death is better than a lifetime chained to a demon."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Intervention, v_current_scene = Ch9-Intervention)


ID: Ch9-Fused
TEXT:

 "Actually, their souls are fused, so Flower didn't technically lie yesterday."

"Damn it, Barry!" Daren snaps, knuckles white on his hilt. "You know exactly what I mean! Two wills in one skin. Without you and Rose keeping watch, what stopped her from drawing a blade across our throats while we slept?"

"I have no need for petty assassination against disarmed mortals," Illuna says coldly. "You and your companions pose zero threat to me in your current state, healer. If you wish to test that assessment, take another step."

"I accept the invitation," Daren says, drawing his sword with a clean rasp of steel. "Let's see if your magic matches your arrogance."

"Daren, stop!" I bark, stepping squarely between them. "Are you planning to execute the girl just to get at the banshee?"

