ID: Ch9-Love
TEXT:

 "If I wanted you dead I'd have let Daren finish the argument."

Illuna's brow knits into a sharp scowl.

"One warning, human, and one only. Do not mistake me for an ally. This arrangement dissolves the moment those collars drop. Expect nothing more."

She slips inside and laces the flap shut.

"Good night, Illuna!" Rose calls out cheerfully. No answer comes from within.

Rose turns to me, smiling in the moonlight. "I'd say that went remarkably well."

"You think?"

"Of course! Did you see how her posture eased when you stepped in? Underneath the thorns, she isn't so different from the rest of us."

"I'm starting to hear Flower talking through you."

"There's a bit of Flower in all of us, Barry," Rose says, tapping my chest. "Yours is just buried under five layers of cynicism and dry remarks."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Backpack, v_current_scene = Ch9-Backpack)


ID: Ch9-Backpack
TEXT:

#if(v_ch9_inventory == 0) {
 Rose smiles warmly.

"Thank you for inviting me along tonight. We should do it again."

"Any time," I tell her.

"Good night, Barry."

"Sleep well, Rose."

I crawl into my tent and collapse onto the bedroll. Sleep hits like a hammer. 

A few hours later, the thump of mallet on pegs and the rustle of folding canvas wake me. I step outside into the gray dawn light.

"Up already, Barry?" Hadrik calls out, grinning as he heaves up a corner pole. "I'll take my canvas back, if you don't mind!"

The campsite is already half dismantled. A pale pink streak cuts across the eastern ridge—four days left of the fox's term.

Before packing, I sit down to count my supplies. With Daren's spatial enchantment hiding the interior depth, I have to picture each tool in my mind and reach into the void to pull it out. Crossbow bolts, spare string, rations, flint...

"What on earth are you doing?" Kate's voice asks from above me.

"Counting gear," I say, pulling out another bundle. "I can't see the bottom of the pack, so I'm checking inventory by hand."

Kate stares at me in disbelief. She reaches down, catches my wrist, and raps my knuckles three times against the inner leather rim of the opening. 

A faint green pulse ripples across the fabric, projecting a sharp, glowing manifest into the air above the pack—a visual list of every bolt, potion, and ration tucked inside the pocket.

"There's your inventory," Kate says dryly. "Shove that gear back in and get moving before we leave you behind."

}
#if(v_ch9_inventory == 1) {
 Rose smiles warmly.

"It was wonderful speaking with her. We should do it again."

"Any time," I tell her.

"Good night, Barry."

"Sleep well, Rose."

I crawl into my tent and collapse onto the bedroll. Sleep hits like a hammer. 

A few hours later, the thump of mallet on pegs and the rustle of folding canvas wake me. I step outside into the gray dawn light.

"Up already, Barry?" Hadrik calls out, grinning as he heaves up a corner pole. "I'll take my canvas back, if you don't mind!"

The campsite is already half dismantled. A pale pink streak cuts across the eastern ridge—four days left of the fox's term.

Remembering Rose's comment from last night, I sit down with my pack and start tapping the leather opening, trying to conjure the manifest. After thirty seconds of fruitless poking and muttering, the canvas remains completely inert.

"What on earth are you doing?" Kate asks, stopping beside me.

"Trying to pull up the inventory display Rose mentioned," I grumble. "This enchantment has no clear trigger."

Kate sighs. She reaches down, catches my wrist, and raps my knuckles three times against the inner leather rim of the opening. 

A faint green pulse ripples across the fabric, projecting a sharp, glowing manifest into the air above the pack—a visual list of every bolt, potion, and ration tucked inside the pocket.

"There's your manifest," Kate says. "Now grab a mallet and help me strike this tent. The sun is up and we have ground to cover."

}
choice("Continue", Ch9-Strategy, v_current_scene = Ch9-Strategy)


ID: Ch9-Strategy
TEXT:

 We break camp and hit the trail as the morning mist burns off. Flower leads the way, her step light again, but a heavy silence hangs over the rest of us. For the first half-hour, the only sound is the crunch of boots on dry pine needles.

Flower glances back, noticing the gloom. "Did... something happen while I was under?"

"No," Daren and I say at the exact same instant.

Flower's eyes dart between us. "Right... definitely nothing."

"Barry and Daren had a disagreement last night," Kate says from the rear. "Pay them no mind. They'll survive each other."

By midday, the woodland thins into rocky scrub. Flower raises a hand, halting us in the shade of an overhanging limestone ledge.

"We're entering their perimeter," she says in a low whisper.

Daren unclasps his shield and sets it against a boulder. "We need to settle our approach. First order of business: what to do with Rose. Either she comes inside under our escort, or we leave her in a secure position out here with a guard. Thoughts?"

"I volunteer to stay with Rose," Kate says immediately. "Without ice magic, my utility in a fortified assault is limited. I can protect her from wandering scouts with hand-to-hand and blades."

choice("Try to convince them that it's safer to take Rose with us", Ch9-Take-Rose, v_current_scene = Ch9-Take-Rose, v_ch9_take_rose = 1)
choice("Agree with Kate's plan", Ch9-Take-Rose, v_current_scene = Ch9-Take-Rose, v_ch9_take_rose = 0)


ID: Ch9-Take-Rose
TEXT:

#if(v_ch9_take_rose == 1) {
 "It's safer to keep Rose with us," I say. "The briefing mentioned ogre mages. If a scouting party brings even one elementalist, Kate has no magic to shield Rose with."

"The lad is right," Hadrik rumbles, nodding. "Leaving them out in the open is too much of a gamble. If we kept Rose breathing through the hydra's den, we can keep her safe inside stone walls."

Daren looks to Rose. "Are you willing, Rose?"

"Of course," she says steadily. "We've weathered worse than this. I won't get in anyone's way."

Hadrik scratches his beard. "Before we charge the gates, can we review the cat's briefing one more time? His Common sounded like stones rattling in an iron pot."

"The objective is a golden chalice with triangular engravings along the rim," Kate says crisply. "It infuses water with restorative magic capable of mending ailments that resist standard healing spells. As for the garrison: regular warriors armed with heavy clubs, and mages wielding elemental spells. The two high-priority targets are their shaman, identifiable by dense ritual tattoos across his torso, and the fortress captain, who wears full plate armor."

"If this chalice is so critical," Hadrik asks, "why doesn't the golden fox flatten the stronghold herself? She's supposed to be a living terror."

"Eleya's power is anchored to the sacred woods," Flower explains. "Outside the groves, she is barely stronger than a normal beast. That is why the war between the animals and monsters has ground on for decades, and why entering the sacred woods is forbidden to outsiders."

"Which leaves the task to us," Daren says. "The fort crowns a bare hill with a full mile of cleared timber around it. Sneaking across open dirt is impossible."

}
#if(v_ch9_take_rose == 0) {
 "I agree with Kate," I say. "Taking Rose into a fortified stronghold packed with ogres while we're collared is too high a risk."

"Aye," Hadrik grunts. "Kate can crack a few ogre ribs with her bare hands if trouble comes sniffing."

Daren reaches into his belt pouch and turns to Rose. "Do you still have the transceiver?"

"Right here," Rose says, patting her satchel. "Do you need it back?"

"Keep it," Daren says. "If anything moves in the brush while we're inside, contact us immediately. We will fight our way back out to you."

Hadrik scratches his beard. "Before we charge the gates, can we review the cat's briefing one more time? His Common sounded like stones rattling in an iron pot."

"The objective is a golden chalice with triangular engravings along the rim," Kate says crisply. "It infuses water with restorative magic capable of mending ailments that resist standard healing spells. As for the garrison: regular warriors armed with heavy clubs, and mages wielding elemental spells. The two high-priority targets are their shaman, identifiable by dense ritual tattoos across his torso, and the fortress captain, who wears full plate armor."

"If this chalice is so critical," Hadrik asks, "why doesn't the golden fox flatten the stronghold herself? She's supposed to be a living terror."

"Eleya's power is anchored to the sacred woods," Flower explains. "Outside the groves, she is barely stronger than a normal beast. That is why the war between the animals and monsters has ground on for decades, and why entering the sacred woods is forbidden to outsiders."

"Which leaves the task to us," Daren says. "The fort crowns a bare hill with a full mile of cleared timber around it. Sneaking across open dirt is impossible."

}
choice("Continue", Ch9-Sloppy, v_current_scene = Ch9-Sloppy)


ID: Ch9-Sloppy
TEXT:

 "Could we negotiate a trade?" Rose suggests. "Offer gold for the chalice?"

"Pointless," Kate says. "The ogres took the artifact knowing its value. They won't part with it for coin."

Hadrik's mouth splits into a broad grin. "Front door it is, then!"

"We have no other viable path," Daren concedes.

"Leave the elementalists to me," Hadrik says, rolling his broad shoulders. "Shapeshifting requires techniques to compensate for a lack of defensive wards. I can compress the aura around my fists until it's as dense as dwarven steel. When they pitch fireballs at us, I'll punch them right back down their throats."

"My broadsword is suited for casters as well," Daren says, tapping the scabbard. "The steel absorbs elemental magic, and my striking technique can sever a caster's link to the magical plane for half an hour."

"There you have it!" Hadrik laughs. "Who needs spells? We'll take their fort by dinner."

"Let's stay grounded," Daren says, checking his straps. "We don't know the layout or the troop distribution. We will have to read the field as we breach. If everyone is ready, let's move."

We advance through the brush until the trees give way to rocky slopes. Under the cover of a rocky hollow, we dig a shallow trench in the loam to cache our bulky packs so they won't hinder us in close quarters. I make sure to pull my crossbow and a quiver of steel-tipped bolts from the canvas before covering the hole with brush.

Flower and Hadrik belly-crawl to the ridge to scout the fort. When they slip back thirty minutes later, their faces are grim.

"It's dead quiet," Hadrik whispers. "No perimeter patrols, no sentries on the ramparts, nothing. It doesn't make sense. Ogres are dull, but nobody leaves a fortress completely unstaffed."

choice(""Maybe they're onto us and want to lure us into a trap."", Ch9-Trap, v_current_scene = Ch9-Trap)
choice(""Empty posts at this hour means they want us inside, or something already killed them."", Ch9-Lunch, v_current_scene = Ch9-Lunch)
choice(""Maybe they abandoned the fort and moved elsewhere?"", Ch9-Moved, v_current_scene = Ch9-Moved)


ID: Ch9-Trap
TEXT:

 "Maybe they're onto us and want to lure us into a trap."

"Likely," Daren says, tightening his grip on his shield. "With competent mages among them, they probably have perimeter wards along the hill. We don't have the luxury of waiting them out. The allotted days are slipping past, and we won't understand their deployment until we step through the gates. Let's pray our lack of internal reconnaissance doesn't cost us our lives."

"Don't worry!" Flower chimes in with a bright smile. "Petal and I have a secret plan. It's foolproof!"

Daren turns a weary look on her. "Do you have any intention of sharing this foolproof plan with the people risking their necks?"

"Of course not!" Flower looks appalled. "How would it be a secret if I told everyone?"

choice("Continue", Ch9-Worry, v_current_scene = Ch9-Worry)


ID: Ch9-Lunch
TEXT:

 "Empty posts at this hour means they want us inside, or something already killed them."

Hadrik snorts. "My coin is on a trap. If they dropped sentry duty for anything less than a siege, the beasts would have cleared them years ago."

"I agree," Daren says, tightening his grip on his shield. "With competent mages among them, they probably have perimeter wards along the hill. We don't have the luxury of waiting them out. The allotted days are slipping past, and we won't understand their deployment until we step through the gates. Let's pray our lack of internal reconnaissance doesn't cost us our lives."

"Don't worry!" Flower chimes in with a bright smile. "Petal and I have a secret plan. It's foolproof!"

Daren turns a weary look on her. "Do you have any intention of sharing this foolproof plan with the people risking their necks?"

"Of course not!" Flower looks appalled. "How would it be a secret if I told everyone?"

choice("Continue", Ch9-Worry, v_current_scene = Ch9-Worry)


ID: Ch9-Moved
TEXT:

 "Maybe they abandoned the fort and moved elsewhere."

Hadrik shakes his head. "Doubt it. The leopard said they slaughtered a goblin clan to claim these stone walls. You don't spill blood for a fortress and abandon it the next month. Smells like an ambush."

"I agree," Daren says, tightening his grip on his shield. "With competent mages among them, they probably have perimeter wards along the hill. We don't have the luxury of waiting them out. The allotted days are slipping past, and we won't understand their deployment until we step through the gates. Let's pray our lack of internal reconnaissance doesn't cost us our lives."

"Don't worry!" Flower chimes in with a bright smile. "Petal and I have a secret plan. It's foolproof!"

Daren turns a weary look on her. "Do you have any intention of sharing this foolproof plan with the people risking their necks?"

"Of course not!" Flower looks appalled. "How would it be a secret if I told everyone?"

choice("Continue", Ch9-Worry, v_current_scene = Ch9-Worry)


ID: Ch9-Worry
TEXT:

#if(v_ch9_take_rose == 0) {
 Daren looks at each of us in turn, his expression grim.

"Right. This will be ugly. We are outnumbered, fighting on unfamiliar ground, stripped of our casting. We've clawed through terrible odds since this tournament began, but don't get reckless. Whatever you do, don't get yourselves killed."

The warning hangs heavy in the clearing. Rose's fingers tighten into the fabric of her skirt. Kate's jaw sets like slate. The sheer lunacy of charging a fortress of hundreds of ogres without magic settles cold in everyone's chest.

Hadrik breaks the silence with a hearty thump to Daren's shoulder pauldron, the steel ringing in the quiet.

"You worry too much, Daren! Nobody's dying today. We didn't survive dragons and hydras just to get laid out by overgrown baboons in a stone pen. We crack some skulls, grab the cup, and brag about it over fireside ale tonight!"

Daren lets out a slow breath. "Let's hope your optimism holds."

He turns to Rose and Kate.

"Rose, you remain with Kate under the tree line. If anything stirs in the brush, trigger the transceiver. We will fight our way out to you."

"We'll be fine, Daren," Rose says softly. "Just come back in one piece."

Kate turns to Flower. "Are you certain about this? The fox only tasked you with leading us to the perimeter. You have fulfilled your obligation. You don't need to step inside."

"I could never leave you to face them alone!" Flower insists, puffing out her chest. "Besides, Petal and I have fought ogres before. They're big and loud, but they aren't that scary."

}
#if(v_ch9_take_rose == 1) {
