choice("Try to convince Flower to open the amulet", Ch9-Open, v_current_scene = Ch9-Open)
choice("Ignore Arraka", Ch9-Ignore, v_current_scene = Ch9-Ignore)


ID: Ch9-Open
TEXT:

#if(v_ch9_ogres != 3) {
 "Flower," I say. "They already know we're inside. She might be trying to flag their positions. Open it."

Flower hesitates, then clicks the clasp.

"They're invisible, you brainless apes!" Arraka's screech splits the air. "They have you encircled! Shoot! Shoot now!"

I raise my crossbow and loose a bolt into the empty yard ahead.

The shaft snaps forward and embeds itself in thin air with a wet thud. Dark blood trickles down the wood. The air ripples, and an eight-foot gray-skinned brute materializes clutching his belly, tusks bared in shock.

All around the courtyard, the shimmer shatters. Over a hundred ogres fade into view, walling us in on every side. Two stand out from the mob: the shaman, lean and corded with dense black ritual tattoos, leaning on a gnarl-wood staff; and the fortress captain, clad head-to-toe in beaten iron plate. The rest are leathery brutes in loincloths, brandishing heavy oak clubs.

}
#if(v_ch9_ogres == 3) {
 "Flower," I say. "They already know we're inside. She might be trying to flag their positions. Open it."

Flower hesitates, then clicks the clasp.

"They're invisible, you brainless apes!" Arraka's screech splits the air. "They have you encircled! Shoot! Shoot now!"

I raise my crossbow and loose a bolt into the space in front of me.

The shaft embeds itself in thin air with a wet thud. The air ripples, and another eight-foot brute materializes clutching his belly, tusks bared in shock.

All around the courtyard, the shimmer shatters. Over a hundred ogres fade into view, walling us in on every side. Two stand out from the mob: the shaman, lean and corded with dense black ritual tattoos, leaning on a gnarl-wood staff; and the fortress captain, clad head-to-toe in beaten iron plate. The rest are leathery brutes in loincloths, brandishing heavy oak clubs.

}
choice("Continue", Ch9-Brave, v_current_scene = Ch9-Brave)


ID: Ch9-Ignore
TEXT:

#if(v_ch9_ogres == 3) {
 We push deeper into the yard. The amulet shakes so violently against Flower's collarbone that the chain rattles.

"What are they waiting for?" Hadrik mutters, scanning the empty stones.

"An opening," Daren says. "Unless..." He stiffens, his eyes widening. "By the gods. They're invisible, like the scouts! Barry, fire the bow! Anywhere, now!"

I snap the crossbow forward and squeeze the trigger.

The bolt embeds itself in empty air with a wet thud. The shimmer tears open, and an eight-foot brute materializes clutching his belly.

All around us, the illusion collapses. Over a hundred ogres appear in a dense ring. The tattooed shaman and the iron-clad captain stand near the center, surrounded by dozens of club-wielding warriors.

}
#if(v_ch9_ogres != 3) {
 We push deeper into the yard. The amulet shakes so violently against Flower's collarbone that the chain rattles, the muffled screeching rising in pitch.

"What are they waiting for?" Hadrik mutters, scanning the empty stones.

"An opening," Daren says, brow furrowed. "If only I could read their deployment..."

The muffled screaming turns frantic enough that words start bleeding through the metal.

"What do you want?!" Flower yells, finally popping the clasp.

"They're invisible, you brainless apes!" Arraka roars. "You're surrounded!"

}
choice("Continue", Ch9-Brave, v_current_scene = Ch9-Brave) if (v_ch9_ogres == 3)
choice("Continue", Ch9-Ogre-club, v_current_scene = Ch9-Ogre-club) if (v_ch9_ogres != 3)


ID: Ch9-Ogre-club
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_reflexes < 3)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_reflexes >= 3)
#if(v_reflexes < 3) {
 

I spin around. 

An eight-foot brute fades into view two feet away, his massive oak club already descending toward my face. My legs try to move but the signal arrives too late—muscles fire a fraction of a second after the window has closed.

A dull, triumphant grin is the last thing I register before fifty pounds of solid timber connects with my skull. The world detonates into white, then collapses to black. I never feel myself hit the ground.

}
#if(v_reflexes >= 3) {
 

I spin around.

An eight-foot brute fades into view two feet away, his massive oak club already descending toward my face.

Instinct takes over. I throw myself sideways as the timber smashes into the cobblestones where I stood, blasting stone chips against my cheek.

I roll to my feet. The ogre stands there, leathery gray brow furrowed, looking baffled that his swing struck empty air.

All around the yard, the illusion collapses. Over a hundred ogres shimmer into view, enclosing us in a tight circle. The tattooed shaman and the iron-clad captain stand near the center, surrounded by dozens of club-wielding warriors.

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Continue", Ch9-Brave, v_current_scene = Ch9-Brave) if (v_reflexes >= 3)


ID: Ch9-Brave
TEXT:

 The courtyard erupts into guttural roars as the ogres heft their clubs, closing the circle. We draw steel and form a tight defensive ring. Before the front line charges, a deafening bellow from the iron-clad captain cuts through the noise.

The mob falls dead silent.

The circle parts, making way for the shaman. He limps forward on bowed legs, his leathery torso covered in swirling black ritual runes, leaning his weight onto a gnarled wooden staff. He stops five paces out and regards us with an oily, toothy grin.

"Welcome, bold warriors," he says in rough, guttural Common. "Humans rarely walk into our valley unbidden. To what do we owe the pleasure of your company?"

choice(""There's no reason for us to explain ourselves to you, monster!"", Ch9-Monster, v_current_scene = Ch9-Monster)
choice(""We came here seeking the sacred chalice."", Ch9-Sacred, v_current_scene = Ch9-Sacred)
choice(""We came for the chalice. If you meant to kill us for walking in, you'd have started already."", Ch9-Merry, v_current_scene = Ch9-Merry)


ID: Ch9-Monster
TEXT:

 "There's no reason for us to explain ourselves to you, monster!"

The shaman's grin vanishes. "I would measure your words, human. You are in no position to dictate terms."

"Like hell we aren't, you filthy brute!" Daren snarls, raising his shield. "We'll cut through your rabble before you can finish giving the order."

"I see," the shaman says coldly. "Then surrender your steel, intruders. You are our prisoners."

Hadrik rolls his shoulders, knuckles hardening. "That's not how this plays out."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Persuade, v_current_scene = Ch9-Persuade) if (v_ch9_take_rose == 0 && v_ch9_ogres != 3)
choice("Continue", Ch9-Submission, v_current_scene = Ch9-Submission) if (v_ch9_ogres == 3 || v_ch9_take_rose == 1)


ID: Ch9-Sacred
TEXT:

 "We came here seeking the sacred chalice."

The shaman rubs his chin, his yellowed eyes narrowing. "Ah. The golden fox's hounds. Whatever she offered in trade is hardly worth your lives. I could have your bones pulverized with a word, but I prefer not to bleed my warriors without cause. My terms: lay down your quest and turn back now. You leave with whole skins, and we forget this intrusion. What say you?"

"Like hell we leave without the chalice, you filthy brute!" Daren snaps, raising his broadsword. "If you won't surrender it, we'll carve our way to the vaults ourselves."

"I see," the shaman says coldly. "Then surrender your steel, intruders. You are our prisoners."

Hadrik rolls his shoulders, knuckles hardening. "That's not how this plays out."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Persuade, v_current_scene = Ch9-Persuade) if (v_ch9_take_rose == 0 && v_ch9_ogres != 3)
choice("Continue", Ch9-Submission, v_current_scene = Ch9-Submission) if (v_ch9_ogres == 3 || v_ch9_take_rose == 1)


ID: Ch9-Merry
TEXT:

 "We came for the chalice. If you meant to kill us for walking in, you'd have started already."

The shaman's mouth twists into a mocking sneer. "A bold guest. We always have need for quarry labor and ditch diggers. You will serve our fort adequately."

"Like hell we'll shovel stone for you, you filthy brute!" Daren snarls, raising his shield. "We'll cut through your rabble before you can finish giving the order."

"I see," the shaman says coldly. "Then surrender your steel, intruders. You are our prisoners."

Hadrik rolls his shoulders, knuckles hardening. "That's not how this plays out."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Persuade, v_current_scene = Ch9-Persuade) if (v_ch9_take_rose == 0 && v_ch9_ogres != 3)
choice("Continue", Ch9-Submission, v_current_scene = Ch9-Submission) if (v_ch9_ogres == 3 || v_ch9_take_rose == 1)


ID: Ch9-Persuade
TEXT:

 "Perhaps I can offer a more compelling argument," the shaman says, striking the butt of his staff against the pavers.

The air behind him ripples. Two massive ogres materialize from the empty courtyard, each carrying an unconscious, bound figure slung over his shoulder.

"No!" Daren's shout tears from his throat.

Kate and Rose hang limp, thick hemp rope binding their wrists and ankles. A dark contusion marks Kate's temple; they were ambushed in the brush before they could draw steel.

"Drop your weapons," the shaman says, his voice flat as slate. "Or my guards will crack their skulls like dry gourds."

choice("Throw down my weapons", Ch9-Throw, v_current_scene = Ch9-Throw)
choice("Shoot the two ogres holding Kate and Rose with my crossbow", Ch9-Crossbow, v_current_scene = Ch9-Crossbow)


ID: Ch9-Crossbow
TEXT:

 I snap the crossbow up and loose two bolts in rapid succession, aiming for the brutes' eyes.

The ogres react with terrifying speed, batting the iron tips out of the air with their heavy clubs.

"A fatal mistake," the shaman says, gesturing with his staff.

The two brutes clamp their massive gray hands around Kate and Rose's heads and squeeze.

A sickening crunch of shattered bone echoes off the stone walls.

"No!" Daren roars in blind agony, charging forward with his blade held high.

The iron-clad captain steps into his path, swinging a massive war club. Daren catches the impact on his shield, but the sheer kinetic force hurls him twenty feet across the yard, smashing him into the granite wall.

Shock freezes me in place. The sight of their crushed skulls paralyzes my chest. My fault. I fired. I killed them.

A heavy shadow falls over me from behind. Before I can turn, fifty pounds of solid oak crashes into the back of my skull, extinguishing all light.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: Ch9-Throw
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch9_decision,1)
achievement("A wise decision",v_ac_ch9_decision)
 I lower the crossbow and toss it onto the cobblestones, unbuckling my dagger belt and throwing it down beside the bow. Daren's jaw works, muscles bunched in his neck, before he unclasps his shield and drops his broadsword with a heavy metallic clatter. Hadrik and Flower carry no steel.

"A sensible choice," the shaman says, nodding to his guards.

The two brutes dump Kate and Rose unceremoniously onto the cobblestones at our feet.

"Rose!" Daren drops to his knees, catching her by the shoulders. "Rose, open your eyes!"

I crouch beside Kate. A shallow, steady rise lifts her chest, and her pulse beats rhythmically beneath her jaw. A dark swelling behind her left ear marks where the club took her down, but her skull is intact and no blood seeps from her ear canal.

Rose groans, eyelids fluttering as she stirs against Daren's arm.

"Daren?... Where... what happened?"

"Ambushed in the brush," Daren says, his voice tight with guilt. "We are inside the stronghold. We had to yield our weapons."

Rose's eyes widen as she takes in the ring of monsters. "Oh gods... you surrendered because of us? Daren, I'm so sorry..."

"Don't," Daren tells her. "We should never have left you exposed."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Interrupt, v_current_scene = Ch9-Interrupt)


ID: Ch9-Submission
TEXT:

 "If you refuse to cooperate, we will break your limbs until you comply," the shaman says.

"That won't be necessary," Flower's voice rings out from my left.

I turn. Her eyes burn with a cold, glacial blue, and her features are locked in Illuna's rigid composure. Beneath the surface, Flower's warm, crackling presence has vanished entirely—shrunk down to a dormant ember beneath the overwhelming spiritual chill of the banshee.

Illuna raises a hand.

Blinding golden radiance erupts from the cobblestones, knitting together in an instant into a towering cage of thick, shimmering bars that encloses Daren, Hadrik, Rose, Kate, and me.

"You remember the golden fox's binding in the ruins," Illuna says through the bars. "Do not waste your energy battering the metal. The weave is indestructible."

The magic hums with suffocating, immovable density. Without casting, we have no hope of bending the bars.

"What is the meaning of this?!" Daren roars, gripping the golden lattice. "What have you done to the girl?"

"Flower is in stasis," Illuna says coldly. "I have tolerated her childish impulses for one day too many. In fact, I have tolerated them to last a lifetime."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Traitorous, v_current_scene = Ch9-Traitorous)


ID: Ch9-Interrupt
TEXT:

 "Enough sentiment," the shaman says, tapping his staff. "Turn around and place your hands behind your backs for the rope. We will secure you in the lower cells and determine your fate by morning."

"That won't be necessary," Flower's voice rings out from my left.

I turn. Her eyes burn with a cold, glacial blue, and her features are locked in Illuna's rigid composure. Beneath the surface, Flower's warm, crackling presence has vanished entirely—shrunk down to a dormant ember beneath the overwhelming spiritual chill of the banshee.

Illuna raises a hand.

Blinding golden radiance erupts from the cobblestones, knitting together in an instant into a towering cage of thick, shimmering bars that encloses Daren, Hadrik, Rose, Kate, and me.

"You remember the golden fox's binding in the ruins," Illuna says through the bars. "Do not waste your energy battering the metal. The weave is indestructible."

The magic hums with suffocating, immovable density. Without casting, we have no hope of bending the bars.

"What is the meaning of this?!" Daren roars, gripping the golden lattice. "What have you done to the girl?"

"Flower is in stasis," Illuna says coldly. "I have tolerated her childish impulses for one day too many. In fact, I have tolerated them to last a lifetime."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Traitorous, v_current_scene = Ch9-Traitorous)


ID: Ch9-Traitorous
TEXT:

 Illuna turns her back on the cage and faces the shaman.

"Great shaman," she says smoothly. "I am Illuna, an awakened banshee. I offer you my expertise in exchange for unrestricted access to the ancient scrolls in your fortress library. As a token of good faith, I deliver these human infiltrators into your hands."

"You treacherous little—" Daren begins, but the armored captain cuts him off with a guttural roar.

"Bold talk for tiny prisoner!" the captain barks in broken Common. "What make banshee think ogres need your tricks?"

"Hold, captain," the shaman says, raising his hand. "This is no ordinary spirit. This is Illuna of the sacred woods massacre. She slaughtered hundreds of forest guardians in a single morning without a shred of pity. Though rumors claimed she grew soft after entangling her soul with a human brat."

"The child was a useful vessel for raw magic," Illuna says without missing a beat, "but her reckless whims repeatedly brought us to the edge of destruction. Escorting these fools into your stronghold was the final straw. I must excise her soul permanently before her foolishness becomes my undoing."

"And how do you plan to achieve that?" the shaman asks.

"Your library holds records of ancient shamanic severance rituals," Illuna says. "Grant me time to study the scrolls, and I will sever the girl's soul cleanly without harming my own essence."

"And in trade, your allegiance to this fortress?"

"Precisely."

The shaman's mouth twists into a cruel grin. "One demonstration of loyalty first. Execute the prisoners in that cage."

"I cannot," Illuna replies evenly. "Executing them now ensures all our deaths."

"Explain."

"The golden fox bound lessathi death-tethers around their throats before sending them to claim your chalice," Illuna says. "If their hearts stop, the collars detonate with enough concussive force to level this entire fortress and incinerate every warrior in the yard. The fox designed the failsafe to destroy you even if the infiltration failed."

choice("Continue", Ch9-Mechanisms, v_current_scene = Ch9-Mechanisms)


ID: Ch9-Mechanisms
TEXT:

 The shaman strokes his scarred chin, eyes narrowing. "Lessathi craftsmanship. Ancient texts speak of those spiteful self-destruct mechanisms."

He pauses, leaning on his staff.

"What do you propose, then?"

"Patience," Illuna says. "The fox set a five-day term. If they fail to return, she can loose the collars' killing spark from afar. Under that discharge, the explosive payload remains inert."

"Sound reasoning," the shaman grunts. "You hold no sentiment for these mortals. The library is yours, Illuna of the sacred woods. We will settle your formal allegiance once the child's soul is severed. As for the humans, I will channel a mass spatial weave to deposit them directly into our dungeons. Keep your cage steady while I prepare the incantation."

"The cage will hold," Illuna says. "One final precaution: the dwarf possesses the physical mass and raw strength of a Northern giant. Standard cell walls will not contain him. Transport them into the suppression cells on the lower sublevels—the ones inlaid with paralysis wards from when the goblins held the fort."

"You know our stronghold well, banshee," the shaman chuckles. "The paralysis cells it is."

He begins chanting in a low, resonant drone, tracing circles in the dust with his staff. Blinding white light spills across the cobblestones, the spatial resonance vibrating through my teeth and bones.

Hadrik grips the golden bars, muscles bulging against the enchantment. "Traitorous wretch! Selling out my giant's blood to monsters!"

Rose steps to the front of the cage, tears tracking down her cheeks. "Illuna, how could you? I trusted you. Flower trusted you. How could you do this to us?"

Illuna looks through the bars, her blue eyes colder than winter frost.

"You trust too easily, Rose," she says, her voice devoid of regret. "I warned you not to consider me an ally. You should have listened to your healer while you had the chance. Now you pay for your naivety."

The banshee's glacial stare and Rose's tears are the last things I see before the white light detonates, pulling us into deep, silent blackness.

choice("Next chapter", Ch10-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch10-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)
